abstract=r'''
\begin{abstract}
\noindent\textbf{Background.} Antibody developability comprises experimentally distinct properties. A representation useful for hydrophobic retention need not explain self-association, thermal unfolding, or expression. This heterogeneity makes model design consequential when only a few hundred labeled antibodies are available. We introduce \AbDev{}, an assay-aware ensemble combining frozen language-model embeddings, molecular descriptors, subtype metadata, and structural features.

\noindent\textbf{Methods.} Four experts perform best-source selection, assay-specific descriptor masking, two-stream ridge stacking, and mechanistic feature-group mixing. Their predictions are averaged. Development uses GDPa1 ($n=246$) and cluster--isotype cross-validation; GDPa3 is an external panel of 80 antibodies with assay-dependent missing labels. We reproduce the primary model from its expert code, evaluate matched ablations, and compute direct input-feature attributions and conditional residue SHAP through its descriptor pathway.

\noindent\textbf{Results.} Across HIC, AC-SINS, PR-CHO, SMAC, and HAC, \AbDev{} attains the highest recorded mean held-out Spearman correlation among nine evaluated strategies: $.472$, compared with $.452$ for D-Stack, $.444$ for E1, $.307$ for ESM-2, and $.273$ for AbLang2. Reproduction matches all five primary scores to rounding precision. Paired BCa intervals support improvements over both PLM baselines on HIC, SMAC, and HAC. Source removal identifies complementary assay-dependent information, while descriptor-pathway SHAP highlights different residues on a sequence-matched predicted Fv. Applying the unchanged code defaults to five additional assays gives weak or uncertain performance; the ten-assay mean is $.249$.

\noindent\textbf{Conclusions.} Complementary assay-aware experts improve aggregate ranking on the evaluated interaction-related endpoints. Direct attributions explain the fitted primary predictor, with explicitly bounded residue-mapping scope. Weak additional endpoints and historical transfer results define the remaining requirements for broader use.
\end{abstract}
'''
