methods=methods[:stats]+r'''
For each assay, we recompute BCa 95\% confidence intervals from 2,000 paired antibody-level resamples with seed 42. Sample sizes are 79 for HIC, 61 for HAC, 74 for Tm2, and 80 for the other GDPa3 assays. A separate paired BCa bootstrap computes the difference between the primary model and each PLM baseline from the same observed antibodies. The ten displayed pairwise intervals are exploratory and are not multiplicity-adjusted. We do not average per-assay confidence limits to create an interval for a cross-assay mean.

The full ten-assay extension applies the unchanged expert classes, including exclusion of subtype from E1 for Tm1, Tm2, Tonset, and SEC. Assays without a keyword specification use the coded fallback: all descriptor/bio features for A-MGFS and all available mechanistic groups for F-MoE. These additional assays therefore test code-default transfer of the architecture, not separately optimized assay masks. Their newly recomputed values replace heterogeneous historical summaries.

\subsection{Matched ablations and data efficiency}
Each source-removal experiment removes the source from every applicable expert, refits the remaining predictors using GDPa1 alone, and evaluates GDPa3. We define $\Delta\rho=\rho_{\mathrm{full}}-\rho_{\mathrm{reduced}}$, so a negative value means removal improves held-out ranking. In the expert-removal experiment, the remaining three independently fitted expert outputs are averaged. Learning curves sample 20\%, 40\%, 60\%, 80\%, and 100\% of each outer training partition using seed $42+f$ for fold $f$, retain its untouched validation fold, and refit the complete expert procedure. Curves report means over the five folds, not replicate-based uncertainty estimates.

\subsection{Direct primary-model attribution and structure mapping}
Once source choices, feature masks, PCA transforms, and assay-level group weights are fitted, every primary expert is affine in its cached input features. Their average is therefore
\begin{equation}
\hat y_a(x)=b_a+\sum_{s,j}w_{a,s,j}x_{s,j}.
\end{equation}
We recover these coefficients exactly through the fitted scaling, PCA, stream meta-heads, and F-MoE mixtures, and verify predictions against the original expert objects. Input-feature IG relative to the assay's training-feature mean $\mu_a$ is
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{IG}_{a,s,j}(x)=w_{a,s,j}(x_{s,j}-\mu_{a,s,j}),\qquad
\sum_{s,j}\mathrm{IG}_{a,s,j}=\hat y_a(x)-\hat y_a(\mu_a).
\end{equation}
This is also interventional linear SHAP with that background mean~\citep{shap}; it is not correlation-conditioned SHAP. Block contributions sum signed feature IG within a source. The displayed block share is the mean absolute block contribution divided by the sum of those block magnitudes. Named descriptor ranks instead use mean absolute individual-feature IG.

Residue maps use permutation SHAP of the actual primary ensemble's descriptor-dependent prediction. For coalition $S$, residues in $S$ retain their observed identity; other positions are alanine. We recompute the original 165 descriptors, including the original region-numbering procedure, while keeping PLM embeddings, structural bio inputs, and subtype fixed. Marginal output changes are averaged over 32 random permutations and their reverses (64 paths, seed 42). Contributions include every active descriptor path through the four primary experts, without training an explanation surrogate. Each path sums to the descriptor-dependent prediction difference between the observed and all-alanine sequences; mean completeness error is below $10^{-7}$ assay units.

The representative antibody, \texttt{P907-A14-arid-pond-618a6}, is the alphabetically first GDPa3 entry with an available ABodyBuilder3 structure, selected independently of labels or predictions. Its 123-residue VH and 107-residue VL exactly match the PDB chains. The structure surface is rendered as an atomic van der Waals envelope expanded by a 1.4-angstrom probe, meshed at 0.8-angstrom spacing; triangle colors use the residue score of the nearest atom. Signed residue SHAP is projected onto those chains; color scales divide by each assay's 95th percentile absolute attribution and cap at $\pm1$. Alanine is an artificial attribution reference, not a proposed stable antibody. These are direct \emph{conditional descriptor-pathway} attributions of the primary predictor. They do not allocate embedding, metadata, or global structural contributions to residues, and they do not establish effects of fully recomputed sequence mutations. Input-feature IG provides the corresponding accounting of all five sources.

Physicochemical LightGBM SHAP, descriptor-only IG, the chain-aware network, and historical source-stack analyses are retained as explicitly identified supplementary comparisons. They are not substituted for primary-ensemble attributions.
'''

