discussion=r'''
\section{Discussion}
The evidence supports a specific story: molecular information is useful in different ways for different developability assays, and a restrained ensemble combines these differences effectively at the available sample size. The five-assay mean of $.472$ is reproducible from the primary code, exceeds every individual component mean in the evaluated comparison, and is accompanied by assay-level uncertainty. It does not establish that AbDev-X is the best predictor for every antibody property or under every benchmark protocol.

The ablations give this architecture a practical rationale. Structural bio features carry much of the HIC and SMAC signal, AbLang2 contributes to AC-SINS, and PR-CHO illustrates that removing a source can improve held-out ranking. Expert averaging reduces reliance on a single source choice without requiring a large final gate. The recorded optimized gate remained uniform, and the primary evaluation explicitly uses equal weights. Separately tested soft routing and mechanistic constraints do not provide consistent improvements (Supplementary Figure~\ref{fig:soft}); these results concern their tested implementations, not the impossibility of better gating or physical priors.

The ten-assay extension changes the interpretation of breadth. The architecture can generate ten outputs, but weak thermal, SEC, and titer results show that computational coverage is not equivalent to validated assay coverage. Applying the same code defaults is a reproducible diagnostic; better endpoint-specific data and carefully validated masks would be needed before claiming a reliable ten-assay predictor. The subset designation is retrospective, so prospective validation should predefine assays and evaluation criteria.

Direct attribution also changes the interpretation of explanatory figures. Feature-space IG now accounts for the actual primary model, and conditional residue SHAP maps its descriptor-dependent prediction onto a matching structure. Global embeddings and structural descriptors do not automatically imply a unique residue allocation. A full end-to-end mutation attribution would require recomputing both PLM encoders, structure-dependent features, and metadata assumptions under a justified reference. The present residue maps are therefore precise pathway explanations rather than substitutes for experimental mutational validation. Their alanine reference, finite permutation sampling, and possible changes in heuristic region annotation should be considered when interpreting individual hotspots.

The historical format-transfer, augmentation, and confidence studies identify additional deployment questions (Supplementary Figures~\ref{fig:transfer}--\ref{fig:learners}). Their source-stack and neural configurations differ from the primary four-expert predictor. Strong bispecific arm-average correlations can be affected by shared parents; uncertain VHH transfer and weak disagreement-based confidence argue for validation on the intended format and candidate population. These results are retained with their actual identities rather than relabeled as primary-model tests.

\section{Conclusions}
AbDev-X combines assay-aware source selection, descriptor masking, two-stream stacking, and mechanistic group prediction through a four-expert average. Code-level reproduction confirms its strongest aggregate result on five interaction-related assays, while matched source and expert ablations explain where the gain arises. Direct primary-model IG and conditional residue SHAP connect predictions to explicit molecular features and highlighted regions of a matching predicted Fv. Broader endpoint accuracy, full sequence-to-structure mutation attribution, calibrated individual uncertainty, and format transfer remain subjects for prospective evaluation.
'''
